  "notes": "Melee: dmg / cd / range / arc / knock. Tools are deliberately poor weapons; `axe`, `pick`, `knife`, `scythe`, `hammer` are what the harvest gates ask for, `chop_mul` is how much better than a fist they are at scenery. The stone tools come before the metal ones so a fresh game finds them first. Guns: real magazines, reload time, spread (radians), bullet speed and life, pellets, pierce, threat per shot and a noise radius. The bow is a gun with a magazine of one and no muzzle flash; it keeps drawing while held.\n\n**Blunt things stagger, edged things bleed, and fists do neither.** That is the whole of the content design for the two, and it is meant to be readable off this table without a tooltip: `stagger` is seconds of interrupted enemy, `bleed` is damage per second left in the wound, and no weapon has both. A missing field means never, the same way a missing `dur` does. The Improvised pipe staggers because it is a length of steel; the Polearm scythe bleeds because it is a blade. `Wear` and `Bleed`/`Stagger` explain the mechanics; this is where the intent lives.\n\n`crit` is the chance this weapon adds to the player's own, and `crit_mul` is how hard its criticals land. They pull in opposite directions on purpose: a rifle or a knife finds the soft parts often and a sledgehammer almost never, but when the sledge does, it is 2.8x. Both are per-weapon because a stone knife and a sledgehammer critting identically was the thing this replaced.\n\n`tier` is the progression band, 1 to 3, and it is a label rather than a gate: tier 1 is the first hour, tier 2 is the workbench, tier 3 is the upgraded bench and what the military left. Mostly it tracks the bench a recipe needs, and the Steel Pipe is the deliberate exception - a bench-1 craft that is tier 1 because it is the starting weapon. The rule the table is held to is that every weapon class has something craftable in every tier it occupies, so no line dead-ends in a weapon you can only find.\n\n**An upgrade is a level on the item, not a row in this table.** A weapon instance carries its level on the Slots stack beside its condition, so the table stays one row per weapon and a save carries one more integer rather than six times the ids. Levels run 1 to 6 and are bought at the bench that makes the weapon, each costing a share of that weapon's own recipe; 4 to 6 also cost Precision Parts, which only come out of an instance past its boss, so the gate is the material and there is no flag to forget. A level is +8% `dmg` and +10% `dur` and touches nothing else on purpose: `cd`, `range`, `arc`, `stagger` and `bleed` are what make a weapon itself, and letting a level move them would turn every weapon into six weapons and blur the shapes this table exists to keep apart. A level makes a weapon more of itself, never something different. **Only a weapon with a recipe takes levels.** A found-only weapon is a fixed point: excellent when you find it, and eventually passed by the crafted line, which is what keeps the bench worth returning to and makes \"a fully upgraded craftable beats a base found special\" true by construction rather than by careful tuning. Loot does not dead-end on it - a found Baseball Bat is what a Spiked Bat is made from, so the way to upgrade what you find is to build with it. The two boss drops carry no `tier`: tier tracks the bench a recipe needs, and they have no recipe.",
